@page "/options/extensions/tutorial"
@using Seneschal.Dev
@inject DevMode Dev
@inject DotnetSdk Sdk

<PageShell Title="Create an Extension" Icon="@Icons.Material.Outlined.School">
<div class="tutorial">
    <p class="tutorial-lead">An extension adds one or more tools to Seneschal. It is a Razor class library built against the small <code>Seneschal.Sdk</code> and shared as a single <code>.snx</code> file.</p>

    <h2 class="opt-group">The Quick Way</h2>
    <p class="tutorial-text">Open the assistant in the corner and choose <i>Help me create a new extension</i>. It asks what the tool should do, writes it, builds it, checks it against the design rules and loads it. Ask again to change what you do not like.</p>

    <h2 class="opt-group">By Hand</h2>
    @if (Dev.Enabled || Sdk.Available)
    {
        <ol class="tutorial-steps">
            <li><span>Open <MudLink Href="/options/projects">Options, Extension Projects</MudLink> and press <i>New Project</i>.</span></li>
            <li><span>Give it a name, for example <code>RegexTester</code>, and press <i>Create</i>. It is built and loaded for you.</span></li>
            <li><span>Find the tool in the sidebar. Edit its files and press <i>Build</i> in Options, Extension Projects, or run <code>dotnet build</code> yourself. Each build is loaded the moment it ends.</span></li>
        </ol>
    }
    else
    {
        <ol class="tutorial-steps">
            <li><span>Install the .NET SDK. Running <code>seneschal dev install</code> in a terminal does it for you alone, without administrator rights.</span></li>
            <li><span>Open <MudLink Href="/options/projects">Options, Extension Projects</MudLink> and press <i>New Project</i>.</span></li>
            <li><span>Build it, and the tool appears in the sidebar. To share it, send the <code>.snx</code> file it produces and import it with <i>Import</i> in <MudLink Href="/options/extensions">Options, Extensions</MudLink>.</span></li>
        </ol>
    }

    <h2 class="opt-group">Reference</h2>
    <MudExpansionPanels MultiExpansion="true" Class="tutorial-panels" Elevation="0">

        <MudExpansionPanel Text="Project">
            <p class="tutorial-text">A Razor class library with a reference to the SDK. In your own repository add the <code>Seneschal.Sdk</code> package and its build rules; the project file stays tiny.</p>
            <pre class="tutorial-code">@ProjectSnippet</pre>
            <pre class="tutorial-code">@FolderSnippet</pre>
        </MudExpansionPanel>

        <MudExpansionPanel Text="Manifest">
            <p class="tutorial-text"><code>extension.json</code> says what the extension is, and the app reads it without loading any code. <code>sdk</code> is the SDK version it was built against. The app accepts the same major version and anything not newer than its own.</p>
            <pre class="tutorial-code">@ManifestSnippet</pre>
        </MudExpansionPanel>

        <MudExpansionPanel Text="Tool">
            <p class="tutorial-text">One small class per tool, and the app finds it on its own. A group is optional, and a tool without one is listed under <i>Other</i>. An extension can hold several tools, and each gets its own sidebar entry and its own on and off switch.</p>
            <pre class="tutorial-code">@ToolSnippet</pre>
            <p class="tutorial-text"><code>Persistent</code> is off by default, so the tool starts fresh each time it opens. Turn it on to keep the view alive while you visit other pages. The state stays in memory and is lost on a restart, or when the tool is turned off or updated.</p>
        </MudExpansionPanel>

        <MudExpansionPanel Text="View and styles">
            <p class="tutorial-text">The view is an ordinary Blazor component using MudBlazor. The app already draws the page title and the favorites button, so the view holds only the tool's own content.</p>
            <p class="tutorial-text">Static files go in <code>wwwroot</code>, and stylesheets listed under <code>styles</code> in the manifest load on every page. Use the theme variables so the tool follows light and dark mode, and give your own class names a specific prefix such as <code>rt-</code> so they never collide with other extensions.</p>
            <pre class="tutorial-code">--fg  --fg-dim  --border  --hover  --accent  --card  --card-border  --tile  --field</pre>
            <p class="tutorial-text">The design rules are in the UI guidelines. The assistant reads them, and <code>seneschal_extension_check</code> compares a project against them.</p>
        </MudExpansionPanel>

        <MudExpansionPanel Text="Services and startup work">
            <p class="tutorial-text">A tool that needs its own services or background work implements <code>IToolLifecycle</code> next to <code>IToolModule</code>. <code>ConfigureServices</code> registers services and <code>Started</code> runs once after the app has started. The context gives the extension id and a private <code>DataDirectory</code> for its files.</p>
            <p class="tutorial-text">Services live in a container of the extension's own, which is replaced when it is rebuilt and disposed when it is removed. Every extension loads, updates and unloads live, and nothing needs a restart.</p>
            <pre class="tutorial-code">@LifecycleSnippet</pre>
        </MudExpansionPanel>

        <MudExpansionPanel Text="Let the AI use it">
            <p class="tutorial-text">Everything the page lets the user do should also be a tool for the assistant. Implement <code>IToolMcp</code> next to <code>IToolModule</code> and list one tool for each thing the user can do: list, create, delete, run. The app offers them to Claude through MCP while the tool is turned on.</p>
            <p class="tutorial-text">The effect decides how a call is treated. <code>Read</code> only looks, <code>Change</code> is easy to undo, and <code>Destructive</code> removes something and always asks the user first.</p>
            <pre class="tutorial-code">@McpSnippet</pre>
            <p class="tutorial-text">Throw <code>McpException</code> with a message the AI can act on, and keep names short and unique, such as <code>regex_tester_test</code>.</p>
        </MudExpansionPanel>

        <MudExpansionPanel Text="NuGet packages">
            <p class="tutorial-text">Add a <code>PackageReference</code> as usual. The build copies the libraries your extension needs into the package and leaves out what the app already provides, which is the SDK and MudBlazor.</p>
            <p class="tutorial-text">Two extensions using the same library each carry their own copy, so they cannot disturb each other. Packages with native files (<code>runtimes</code>) are included but cannot be unloaded, so removing or updating them may need a restart.</p>
        </MudExpansionPanel>

        <MudExpansionPanel Text="Build and share">
            <p class="tutorial-text"><code>dotnet build</code> stages the extension as a folder and as a package.</p>
            <pre class="tutorial-code">@BuildSnippet</pre>
            <p class="tutorial-text">The folder is what Live Reload watches. To use an extension somewhere else, import its <code>.snx</code> in Options, Extensions. A <code>.zip</code> of the same files and a single <code>.dll</code> without a manifest are accepted too.</p>
        </MudExpansionPanel>

        <MudExpansionPanel Text="Rules">
            <ul class="tutorial-list">
                <li>Depend on <code>Seneschal.Sdk</code>, MudBlazor and your own packages.</li>
                <li>Components inside the app, such as the page frame, are not available to extensions.</li>
                <li>A tool <code>Id</code> must be unique, and a clash with another extension is rejected.</li>
                <li>An extension built for another major SDK version is rejected with a message that says so.</li>
                <li>A package runs with full access to the machine, so import only packages you trust.</li>
            </ul>
        </MudExpansionPanel>
    </MudExpansionPanels>
</div>
</PageShell>

@code {
    private const string ProjectSnippet = """
        <Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">
          <PropertyGroup>
            <RootNamespace>RegexTester</RootNamespace>
            <ExtensionId>regex-tester</ExtensionId>
          </PropertyGroup>
        </Project>
        """;

    private const string FolderSnippet = """
        extensions/RegexTester/
          RegexTester.csproj
          extension.json          the manifest
          RegexTesterTool.cs      the tool: id, title, group, icon
          RegexTesterView.razor   the UI (content only)
          RegexTesterEngine.cs    the code that does the work (no UI, easy to test)
          wwwroot/regextester.css optional static files
        """;

    private const string ManifestSnippet = """
        {
          "id": "regex-tester",
          "name": "Regex Tester",
          "version": "1.0.0",
          "description": "Try regular expressions against sample text",
          "entry": "RegexTester.dll",
          "sdk": "1.0",
          "styles": [ "regextester.css" ]
        }
        """;

    private const string ToolSnippet = """
        public sealed class RegexTesterTool : IToolModule
        {
            public ToolMetadata Metadata { get; } = new(
                Id: "regex-tester",
                Title: "Regex Tester",
                Description: "Try regular expressions against sample text",
                Group: "Text",                // optional: empty means "Other"
                Icon: Icons.Material.Filled.Spellcheck,
                ComponentType: typeof(RegexTesterView),
                SearchTerms: ["regex", "pattern", "match"])
            {
                Persistent = true,            // optional: keep the tool's state while you visit other pages
            };
        }
        """;

    private const string LifecycleSnippet = """
        public sealed class RegexTesterTool : IToolModule, IToolLifecycle
        {
            // ... Metadata as above ...

            public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services, IExtensionContext context)
            {
                services.AddSingleton(new RegexTesterStore(context.DataDirectory));
            }

            public void Started(IServiceProvider services, IExtensionContext context)
            {
                // runs once, after the app has started
            }
        }
        """;

    private const string McpSnippet = """
        public sealed class RegexTesterTool : IToolModule, IToolMcp
        {
            // ... Metadata as above ...

            public IEnumerable<McpTool> McpTools(IServiceProvider services)
            {
                yield return new McpTool("regex_tester_test", "Test a pattern against some text",
                    McpEffect.Read,
                    McpSchema.Object()
                        .Text("pattern", "The regular expression", required: true)
                        .Text("text", "The text to test", required: true),
                    args => Regex.IsMatch(args.Text("text"), args.Text("pattern")));
            }
        }
        """;
    private const string BuildSnippet = """
        dotnet build extensions/RegexTester

        artifacts/extensions/regex-tester/     the folder the app loads (dev builds)
        artifacts/packages/regex-tester.snx    the package to import elsewhere
        """;
}
